  "preamble": "Debuccalization of the uvular ejective: the oral closure of [qʼ] is lost and only the glottal closure remains, giving [ʔ]. In Megrelian (Zan Kartvelian), Proto-Kartvelian *qʼ usually became ʔ (written ჸ) word-initially, between vowels and before v, while Georgian and Svan keep qʼ; after another consonant, especially in ejective clusters, qʼ survives, and some stems still vary (qʼ ~ ʔ). Tsagareli (1880) already noted that Georgian qʼ 'in most cases' corresponds to Megrelian ʔ. Independently, stem-final *qʼ became ʔ in Navajo and other tonal Athabaskan languages; Ahtna keeps it in kaekʼ 'track' and kaakʼe 'on top', but Central and Lower Ahtna have ʔ in 'three' (taaʼi, beside Western taakʼi). In Circassian (Northwest Caucasian), Proto-Circassian *qʼ and *qʷʼ became ʔ and ʔʷ in nearly all Adyghe and Kabardian dialects; only Hakuchi Shapsug keeps qʼ (Chirikba 1996).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Debuccalization, Lenition",
